   267	\min\left\{
   268	N^{1/6}R^{1/6},\,
   269	N^{1/4}R^{-1/4}
   270	\right\}N^{o(1)}.
   271	}
   272	\]
   273	At \(R=N^\rho\), its exponent is
   274	\[
   275	\begin{cases}
   276	(1+\rho)/6,&0\le\rho\le1/5,\\
   277	(1-\rho)/4,&1/5\le\rho\le1/3.
   278	\end{cases}
   279	\]
   280	The maximum is \(1/5\), at \(\rho=1/5\). This improves extraction and simplifies the construction; it does not remove the ratio penalty.
   281	
   282	**3. The curvature lemma is correct under explicit assumptions; its proposed universal interpretation is not.**
   283	
   284	Let
   285	\[
   286	G(x)=\frac{B(r+Mx)+AN/(r+Mx)-c}{M^2}.
   287	\]
   288	For positive integer \(A\),
   289	\[
   290	G''(x)=\frac{2AN}{(r+Mx)^3}
   291	\ge\frac{2R}{P}
   292	\]
   293	throughout the smaller-factor interval.
   294	
   295	If \(G''\ge\kappa>0\), the preimage of a vertical interval of width \(m\) has at most two monotone components. Each has length at most \(\sqrt{2m/\kappa}\): integrate the second-derivative bound from the endpoint nearest the minimum. Therefore
   296	\[
   297	\boxed{
   298	\#\{x\in\mathbb Z:G(x)\in[a,a+m]\}
   299	\le2\sqrt{mP/R}+2.
   300	}
   301	\]
   302	Your chain-rule cancellation of \(M^2\) is correct. This counts candidates in the residue class \(p=r+Mx\).
   303	
   304	If \(G_{\rm count}\) separately charged windows must cover \(\asymp P\) candidates, then
   305	\[
   306	G_{\rm count}\left(2\sqrt{mP/R}+2\right)\gtrsim P.
   307	\]
   308	In the stated range, optimizing \(m+G_{\rm count}\) gives
   309	\[
   310	\boxed{
   311	m+G_{\rm count}\gtrsim(PR)^{1/3}
   312	=Q^{1/3}=N^{1/6}R^{1/6}.
   313	}
   314	\]
   315	So the calculation is sound **for full-candidate coverage by individually charged consecutive exponent windows**.
   316	
   317	The qualifications are substantial:
   318	
   319	- **Coverage quantifier.** D′ covers the true factor for almost all pairs. It does not cover \(P\) candidates for each fixed input \(N\). Your lower-bound deduction inserts that stronger requirement.
   320	- **Cost model.** Counting windows is not automatically a lower bound on computation. A future algorithm might process a structured family implicitly.
   321	- **Intended versus accidental collisions.** The argument counts real-window coverage. It does not count useful collisions arising through reduction modulo a group order.
   322	- **Allowed targets.** The inequality assumes a nonzero positive integer coefficient of \(N/p\). Zero coefficients, rational normalization, nonlinear handles and other representations require separate arguments.
   323	
   324	For example, \(A=0,B=M\) gives \(T=Mp\), locked modulo \(M^2\) by \(p\bmod M\), and the normalized function is linear. This degenerates to an ordinary progression search; it supplies no faster algorithm, but disproves an unrestricted claim about “any linear-in-\(N\) target.”
   325	
   326	The \(q\)-side does **not** escape the lemma within the positive-weight model. Its target is
   327	\[
   328	T'=Bq+Ap.
   329	\]
   330	Express it again in the smaller factor:
   331	\[
   332	T'(p)=Ap+BN/p.
   333	\]
   334	A residue class for \(q\bmod M\) corresponds bijectively to one for \(p\bmod M\), and
   335	\[
   336	\frac{d^2}{dx^2}\frac{T'(r+Mx)-c'}{M^2}
   337	=\frac{2BN}{p^3}\ge\frac{2R}{P}.
   338	\]
   339	The coefficient roles swap, so the same bound applies.
   340	
   341	My assessment of the suggested escape routes is:
   342	
   343	| Modification | Concrete assessment |
   344	|---|---|
   345	| Composite locks | \(M^2\) still cancels from the second derivative. Multiple root classes must be counted. No improvement follows merely from making \(M\) composite. |
   346	| Both-side extraction | Removes extraction failures, as above; it does not shrink these windows. |
   347	| Rational weights | Clearing denominators restores integer coefficients and exponent scaling. Taking fractional group powers needs an additional justified operation. |
   348	| Multipliers \(kN\) | Balancing by \(k\asymp R\) naturally incurs \((kN)^{1/6}\asymp N^{1/6}R^{1/6}\). This reproduces the penalty. |
   349	| HH local lattices | With \(S=Mm_0\), their usual accounting is \(S+\sqrt N/S^{3/2}\). The nonzero-\(A\) regime has \(A=O(\sqrt{S/R})\); optimizing recovers \(N^{1/5}\), without a ratio improvement below the crossover. |
   350	| McKee–Pinch pigeonhole | Guarantees suitable weighted congruences, but its proved accounting does not yield the desired \(1/6\) exponent. |
   351	| Nonlinear \(E(N)\), higher-degree lattices, other groups | Outside this curvature proof. I found no rigorous construction attaining the requested uniform bound. They remain loopholes in an impossibility claim. |
   352	
   353	The HH lattice window and coefficient bounds follow directly from their Proposition 3.3. [Harvey–Hittmeir 2022](https://arxiv.org/html/2105.11105)
   354	
   355	Thus: the lemma explains the \(R^{1/6}\) penalty in this model. It does not establish that every deterministic or almost-all factoring algorithm must pay it.
   356	
   357	**4. I found no published result supplying the missing uniform ratio theorem.**
   358	
   359	The relevant literature distinguishes rigorous bounds from heuristic speedups:
   360	
   361	| Work | Relevant result |
   362	|---|---|
   363	| [Lehman 1974](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1974-0340163-2) | Rational approximations to the factor ratio give a deterministic \(N^{1/3}\) algorithm; no intermediate-ratio sub-\(1/5\) theorem follows. |
   364	| [McKee 1996, Euler method](https://doi.org/10.1112/blms/28.4.351) | Proved \(N^{1/3+\eta}\) algorithm. |
   365	| [McKee 1999, speeding Fermat](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=010e639e5176f16cee1b8aff586141f4d5c8eb10&repid=rep1&type=pdf) | The \(N^{1/4+\eta}\) speedup is heuristic. |
   366	| [McKee–Pinch 1996, §5](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-61581-4_57) | The weighted congruence gives cost \(\widetilde O(\max(\lambda p,\mu q)/k^2)\) per lock. Their pigeonhole construction proves \(N^{1/3+\eta}\); the balanced \(1/4\) variant is heuristic. I checked the repository’s scanned pages. |
   367	| [Hittmeir 2020](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16729) | Proved deterministic \(N^{2/9+o(1)}\). |
   368	| [Harvey 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.05450), [Harvey–Hittmeir 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11105) | General deterministic exponent \(1/5\); the latter improves logarithmic factors. |
   369	| [Gao–Feng–Hu–Pan, 2512.19076](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.19076v1) | Their stated factoring theorem assumes \(p,q=\Theta(\sqrt N)\) and improves the logarithmic factor to \(\log^{13/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5}\). It supplies no polynomial-ratio extension. |
   370	| [Harvey–Hittmeir 2026](https://arxiv.org/html/2601.11131) | Removes the lower restriction on the target order \(D\), enabling the extraction combination above. It supplies no ratio-dependent factoring exponent improvement by itself. |
   371	
   372	Carella’s claimed \(1/6\) result has a concrete proof defect. In Theorem 5.1, write the correctly expanded polynomial as
   373	\[
   374	f(x,y)=(P_0+Dx)(Q_0+Dy)-N,
   375	\qquad D\asymp N^{1/6}.
   376	\]
   377	Because \(P_0Q_0\equiv N\pmod D\), **every coefficient is divisible by \(D\)**. The displayed coefficient list omits the factors \(D\) on the linear terms and then asserts primitivity. After removing the content, at \(X=Y=N^{1/3}\),
   378	\[
   379	W\ll N/D=N^{5/6}.
   380	\]
   381	The cited bivariate condition would require
   382	\[
   383	XY<W^{2/3}\ll N^{5/9},
   384	\]
   385	whereas \(XY=N^{2/3}\). Hence that proof establishes neither the balanced claim nor its asserted unbalanced extension. [Carella, equations (17)–(21)](https://arxiv.org/html/1308.2891)
   386	
   387	For \(R=N^\rho\), the concrete verdict is therefore:
   388	
   389	- For \(0<\rho<1/5\), the derivation above gives an almost-all exponent \((1+\rho)/6<1/5\). I found no prior theorem covering every such prescribed semiprime band with the requested uniform \(1/6\) exponent.
   390	- For \(1/5<\rho\le1/3\), Pollard–Strassen already gives the rigorous worst-case exponent \((1-\rho)/4<1/5\).
   391	- At \(R=N^{1/5+o(1)}\), both meet at \(N^{1/5+o(1)}\). None of the checked results removes this peak.
