   277	    them, and at most one is ≡ 0 (mod ℓ) because 2Δ < ℓ. So ω(ℓ) ≥ Δ − 2 distinct classes
   278	    qB^{−1}.
   279	  - Hence |U ∩ I_{q,b}| ≤ (|I_{q,b}| + 1 + Q²)·Cn/Δ. Summing over b costs an extra R·Q²,
   280	    which is ≪ |I_q| because R^{3/2}Y² ≪ √N.
   281	  - Exceptional fraction: C n²/Δ.
   282	- **FULL (Lemma 10′, roles swapped).** Here |A − B| = B − 1 ≍ R, so D′'s per-q count would
   283	  lose a factor R. Instead fix (p, B, ℓ, δ) and count q.
   284	  - In a clean block, FULL means d = ord_q(2) divides X := (B−1)(p−1) + ℓ²δ.
   285	  - For R ≥ 4Δ and n ≥ n₀, 0 < X < 3q, because ℓ²m̄ ≤ 4·span̄ + 8·4^KY² < (R/2)p.
   286	  - So q ≡ 1 (mod e) for some divisor e > T of X, with ℓ ∤ e (clean), q ≡ Bp (mod ℓ), and
   287	    q ∈ (p(B−Δ), p(B+Δ)]. That gives ≤ τ(X)(2Δp/(Tℓ) + 1) values of q.
   288	  - Summing over δ, ℓ, B and p, and dividing by |S_{n,j}|, the fraction is
   289	    ≤ Cn²τ*(m̄Δ/T + m̄RY/√(NR)), with τ* = max_{X<N} τ(X) = N^{O(1/log log N)}.
   290	  - At Y ≍ Δ^{2/3}N^{1/6}R^{−5/6} and Δ ≍ H², this is
   291	    ≤ N^{−1/12+11ε/3+o(1)} + N^{−1/6+4ε/3+o(1)}.
   292	  - No averaging of gcd(B−1, d) is needed. (r16 suggested that averaging; swapping the roles
   293	    avoids it.)
   294	- **Clean block.** Y_j can be as small as ≈ 2Δ = N^{ε+o(1)}. Lemma 6 then needs
   295	  K = ⌈log q/log Y_j⌉ + 1 = O(1/ε) dyadic blocks, which costs a constant factor 2^{O(1/ε)}.
   296	  - The frozen W2 uses K = 4. At the oper2 sizes Y_j⁴ < q for some (n, j); for example
   297	    n = 96, j = 13 has Y = 373, so Y⁴ ≈ 2^{34} against q ≈ 2^{55}.
   298	  - A clean block is then not guaranteed. `certified` checks cleanliness directly.
   299	- **Variant U_θ′ (proved).** For θ ≤ 1/5 − 3ε, the statement of Theorem U_θ holds with W2 in
   300	  place of §3.1, with Δ = ⌊h/(64·2^{K})⌋, K = ⌈3/(5ε)⌉ + 2 and a constant C_ε = 2^{O(1/ε)}.
   301	  The coarse design is simpler and has no such constant, so it is the main theorem.
   302	
   303	### 3.3 Exponent profile with Pollard–Strassen
   304	
   305	**Pollard–Strassen (prior art).**
   306	- Deterministic. It finds every prime factor ≤ z of N in O(z^{1/2}(log N)^{O(1)}) bit
   307	  operations (Pollard 1974; Strassen 1976; Bostan–Gaudry–Schost 2007; Harvey 2021,
   308	  Prop. 2.5).
   309	- A pair of ratio ≥ N^{θ} has p ≤ N^{(1−θ)/2}. So PS costs N^{(1−θ)/4} in the **worst
   310	  case**, with no exceptions.
   311	
   312	**Corollary P (proved).** For each θ ∈ [0, 1] there is a deterministic algorithm that
   313	factors all but O(N^{−ε}) of the pairs with ratio in (N^{θ}/2, 2N^{θ}] at cost
   314	N^{e(θ)+O(ε)}, where
   315	
   316	  e(θ) = min((1+θ)/6, (1−θ)/4).
   317	
   318	The DCL branch is valid only for θ ≤ 1/5 − 3ε; PS is valid for any θ.
   319	- e(θ) > 1/6 on (0, 1/3).
   320	- max e = 1/5, at θ = 1/5.
   321	- e(θ) ≤ 1/5 − |θ − 1/5|/6 elsewhere.
   322	
   323	**A whole range of ratios** (all q/p ≤ N^{θ₀}) costs the maximum over θ ≤ θ₀:
   324	- for θ₀ ≤ 1/5 − 3ε, (1+θ₀)/6 + O(ε) (Theorem U_θ);
   325	- for θ₀ ≥ 1/5, 1/5 + O(ε). That is no better than Harvey 2021 / Harvey–Hittmeir 2022,
   326	  which give N^{1/5} in the worst case for all N.
   327	
   328	**The ratio gap is real for every scheme we have.** For 0 < θ < 1/3 the cost exceeds
   329	N^{1/6} by N^{min(θ/6, (1/3−θ)/4)}.
   330	
   331	### 3.4 Remark: deterministic extraction via Harvey–Hittmeir 2026 (proved given their Thm 1.1; not used in the theorems)
   332	
   333	**The cited theorem.** Harvey–Hittmeir, arXiv 2601.11131 v2 (5 Jun 2026), Thm 1.1, checked
   334	against the arXiv text: "input integers N ⩾ 3 and D ⩾ 1 with D < N−1 … outputs either
   335	some α ∈ ℤ_N^* with ord_N(α) > D or a nontrivial divisor of N", in
   336	O(D^{1/2}log D (log log D)^{−1/2} log N).
   337	
   338	**The construction (GPT-6.1-sol `unbalanced_1.out` §2).**
   339	- Take D = m and 7 disjoint prime blocks with Y⁷ > N.
   340	- (p−1)(q−1) < N has ≤ 6 prime factors > Y. So some block's L is coprime to
   341	  ord_N(α) | λ(N), and then ord_N(γ) = ord_N(α^L) > m.
   342	- Test gcd(γ^i − 1, N) for i < m. A proper gcd factors N. Suppose ord_N(γ) > m and no gcd is
   343	  proper. Then ord_p(γ), ord_q(γ) ≥ m: otherwise gcd(γ^{ord_p(γ)} − 1, N) is p or N, and
   344	  N would force ord_N(γ) ≤ ord_p(γ) < m.
   345	- Find exact giant–baby equalities mod N by sorting, and test each directly: solve
   346	  Bz² − Tz + AN = 0 for the proposed T = c + ℓ²(g_lo + jm + i).
   347	  - For the true giant the index is unique mod p (ord_p(γ) ≥ m), so the test succeeds.
   348	  - A failed test proves the giant is not the true one.
   349	- Otherwise, f(G) ≡ 0 (mod N) with no exact equality means there is an i with p | G − γ^i
   350	  and q ∤ G − γ^i. Scanning that one giant (O(m)) then gives a proper factor.
   351	
   352	**Consequence (proved, given Thm 1.1).**
   353	- On the clean block, every covering true giant yields a factor.
   354	- E_a and E_c disappear. The exceptional set of Theorems D′, U and U_θ reduces to E_b
   355	  (coverage) alone.
   356	- The extra cost is O(√m·polylog) for α, three more blocks, and O((m+G)log) for the sort.
   357	- The θ restriction then comes only from E_b (θ ≤ 1/5) and from cost.
   358	
   359	We did not implement this, and it is not part of the frozen experiments.
   360	
   361	## 4. Which populations make the result meaningful
   362	
   363	Profile: e(θ) = min((1+θ)/6, (1−θ)/4), with θ = log(q/p)/log N. The functions
   364	`obstruction.e_profile`, `frac_theta_below` and `frac_covered_at` compute it.
   365	
   366	1. **Bounded ratio (S_n, D′) and any fixed band up to N^{o(1)}.** Exponent 1/6 + o(1) with
   367	   N^{−ε}-type exceptions per band (Theorems D′ and U). This is the meaningful
   368	   almost-all-1/6 statement.
   369	2. **Uniform over semiprimes with both primes ≥ N^{1/3}**, counted by pairs (equivalently by
   370	   N ≤ X). By Mertens, the density of p ≈ N^{a} is ∝ da/(a(1−a)). With a = (1−θ)/2, θ has
   371	   density ∝ 1/(1−θ²) on [0, 1/3], so Pr[θ ≤ t] = log₂((1+t)/(1−t)) (proved asymptotically;
   372	   `frac_theta_below`).
   373	   - At cost N^{1/6+η}, only θ ≤ 6η (DCL) and θ ≥ 1/3 − 4η (PS) are handled. That is a
   374	     fraction ≈ 21η/ln 2 ≈ 30η: 0.151 at η = 0.005 and 0.301 at η = 0.01. **So there is no
   375	     almost-all N^{1/6+o(1)} statement for this population.**
   376	   - Exponent 1/5 − η′ fails only on θ ∈ (1/5 − 6η′, 1/5 + 4η′), a fraction ≈ 30η′. So every
   377	     exponent below 1/5 holds for a positive proportion < 1, and none for 1 − o(1).
   378	   - The median θ is (√2−1)/(√2+1) = 0.172, where e = 0.195.
   379	3. **Log-uniform ratio on [1, N^{1/3}]** (θ uniform). Same conclusion, with constants
   380	   10η·3 = 30η.
   381	4. **All semiprimes counted by N ≤ X.**
   382	   - Almost all have p < X^{δ} for every fixed δ: the fraction with p > X^{δ} is
   383	     ≈ log(1/(2δ))/log log X. So PS gives exponent δ/2 with o(1) exceptions, and the
   384	     question is trivial.
   385	   - With **power-saving** exceptions (N^{−ε}) nothing below 1/5 follows. The pairs with
   386	     θ ∈ [1/5 ± η] are a ≍ η/log log X ≫ X^{−ε} fraction.
   387	   - Counted this way, "all but N^{−ε} of all semiprimes at N^{1/6+O(ε)}" is **false for
   388	     every scheme in Proposition O′'s model** (full-cover sense), and not achieved by ours.
   389	
   390	## 5. The curvature obstruction (Proposition O′)
   391	
   392	### 5.1 Model 𝔏 (one-dimensional Lehman-linear collision searches)
   393	
   394	Fix N and a ratio band R₀. The **candidates** are the integers p ∈ [x₁, x₂] with
   395	R₀ ≤ N/p² ≤ 2R₀, so x₂ = ⌊√(N/R₀)⌋ and x₂ − x₁ ≥ x₂/4. A search consists of:
   396	- baby lists Λ_1, …, Λ_s, where Λ_i = {γ_i^e : e ∈ S_i} for an **arbitrary** set S_i ⊂ Z
   397	  with |S_i| = m_i;
   398	- giants G, each compared against one list i(G), and each carrying a target
   399	  τ = (α, β, c₀, M, r, g₀) with integers α, β, c₀, g₀ and modulus M ≥ 1.
   400	
   401	The target's real-valued index on its class is
   402	
   403	  g_τ(p) = (αp + βN/p + c₀)/M²,   p ≡ r (mod M).
   404	
   405	G **covers** a candidate p in its class iff ⌊g_τ(p)⌋ ∈ g₀ + S_{i(G)}.
   406	- For the true factor g_τ is an integer (a lock), so this is exactly "G collides at p if p
   407	  is the factor". For other candidates it is a relaxation, which only enlarges coverage.
   408	- For β = 0, refine the class to the sub-class on which g_τ is integral. A true factor
   409	  outside that sub-class cannot collide, so nothing is lost.
   410	
   411	The **cost** is X = Σ_i m_i + #giants.
   412	
   413	Nothing is assumed about how giants are computed, the group or base, the modulus (prime,
   414	composite, CRT products), the residue class, or which factor is extracted.
   415	- A q-side target αq + βp + c₀ is, in the p-parametrisation, βp + αN/p + c₀: the same form.
   416	- Multipliers kN and rational weights clear to integer (α, β).
   417	
   418	### 5.2 Statement and proof
   419	
   420	**Proposition O′ (proved).**
   421	- (a) If β ≠ 0, a giant with |S| = m covers at most 2m + 8√(m x₂³/(|β|N)) ≤ 2m + 8√(m x₂/R₀)
   422	  candidates.
   423	- (b) If β = 0, it covers at most m.
   424	- (c) Hence if a search in 𝔏 covers at least a fraction f of the candidates, then
   425	
   426	    X ≥ min( √(f x₂)/4, (f/64)^{2/3}(x₂R₀)^{1/3} ) ≍ min(√(fp), f^{2/3}q^{1/3}),
   427	
   428	  where x₂ ≍ p and x₂R₀ ≍ q, both up to a factor √2.
   429	
   430	*Proof of (a).*
   431	- Put p = r + Mx. Then g(x) := g_τ(r+Mx) satisfies g''(x) = 2βN/p³, a chain-rule
   432	  cancellation of M². WLOG β > 0 (otherwise negate g; the half-open floor intervals flip,
   433	  and nothing changes). So g'' ≥ κ := 2βN/x₂³ > 0 on the band.
   434	- Let x₀ minimise g on the band (possibly an endpoint) and m₀ = g(x₀). Consider the right
   435	  part x ≥ x₀; the left part is symmetric.
   436	  - There g′(x) ≥ κ(x − x₀) ≥ 0.
   437	  - Since g′ is increasing, g(x) − m₀ ≤ (x − x₀)g′(x) ≤ g′(x)²/κ, so
   438	    **g′(x) ≥ √(κ(g(x) − m₀))**.
   439	- For an integer v put u = v − m₀ and P_v = {x ≥ x₀ : v ≤ g(x) < v + 1}, an interval.
   440	  - It is empty if u ≤ −1.
   441	  - Its length is ≤ √(2(u+1)/κ), from g − m₀ ≥ κ(x−x₀)²/2.
   442	  - For u > 0 its length is also ≤ 1/√(κu), because g′ ≥ √(κu) on P_v.
   443	  - It contains ≤ length + 1 integers.
   444	- The m values v ∈ g₀ + S give distinct u with integer spacing. At most one lies in (−1, 0]
   445	  (length ≤ √(2/κ)), at most one in (0, 1) (length < 2/√κ), and the i-th value ≥ 1 is ≥ i.
   446	- So the right part covers ≤ m + (√2 + 2 + Σ_{i≤m} i^{−1/2})/√κ ≤ m + 5.5√(m/κ) integers.
   447	- Doubling for the two parts, with √(m/κ) = √(m x₂³/(2βN)):
   448	  2m + 11/√2·√(m x₂³/(βN)) ≤ 2m + 8√(m x₂³/(βN)). Finally x₂³/N ≤ x₂/R₀. ∎(a)
   449	
   450	*Proof of (b).*
   451	- On the refined class, g(x) = g(0) + kx with k = αM′/M² a nonzero integer, where M′ is the
   452	  refined modulus. So each value has at most one preimage. ∎(b)
   453	
   454	*Proof of (c).*
   455	- Let G_i be the number of giants on list i. The coverage is
   456	  ≤ Σ_i G_i(2m_i + 8√(m_i x₂/R₀)) ≤ 2X² + 8√(x₂/R₀)X^{3/2}, since m_i, Σ G_i ≤ X.
   457	- It must be ≥ f x₂/4. If both X² < f x₂/16 and X^{3/2} < f√(x₂R₀)/64, the total is
   458	  < f x₂/4, a contradiction. ∎
   459	
   460	**Corollary O″ (proved, in 𝔏).**
   461	- At ratio R₀ = N^{θ}, full or constant-fraction coverage costs
   462	  ≥ c·N^{min((1−θ)/4, (1+θ)/6)}.
   463	- That is ≥ c N^{1/5} at θ = 1/5, with any number of moduli. Compare:
   464	  - cycloharvey's LB is N^{1/5} with a single modulus;
   465	  - multimod Thm A is N^{1/6} for balanced pairs with many moduli. Prop O′ is its
   466	    ratio-dependent form, and it now allows **arbitrary baby sets**.
   467	- So the profile e(θ) of §3.3 is **optimal in 𝔏 up to N^{O(ε)}**, on both branches.
   468	  - Theorem U_θ covers a constant fraction of the candidates at q^{1/3}N^{O(ε)} (λ ≍ H²/n
   469	    multiplicity).
   470	  - The √p branch is matched by PS. PS is not itself in 𝔏, but it is a β = 0-type cost.
   471	
   472	Tests: `test_prop_o_bound_random` (interval windows, best g₀), `test_prop_o_prime_arbitrary_sets`
   473	(best m-set for one giant = the m most popular floor values). The bounds hold on 700
   474	random targets, with the worst ratios 0.50 (interval) and > 0.1 (sets). For an interval at
   475	the band edge, a single side is in range, so the factor-2 slack is expected.
   476	
   477	### 5.3 Scope (what Proposition O′ does NOT say)
   478	
   479	- **Full cover, not average case.** It bounds how many candidates a search can cover for
   480	  one N. An almost-all algorithm needs only the *true* factor covered for most N.
   481	  - It could beat the bound only if its covered sets C(N) contained the true factor with
   482	    probability well above |C(N)|/#candidates.
   483	  - All lock-class designs have the density property: their classes are N-determined, and
   484	    Lemma 9 is a density statement. But no general theorem excludes correlated designs.
   485	  - Average-case lower bounds: **not proved** (GPT-6.1-sol r16 and unbalanced_1 make the
   486	    same point).
   487	- **Accidental collisions** (mod a reduced group order) are not counted. Heuristically they
   488	  occur at rate ≈ X²/ord_p(γ) ≤ X²/p^{1−o(1)}, which matters only when X ≳ √p, the other
   489	  branch.
   490	- **Outside the model:**
   491	  - targets non-linear in (p, q): exponent polynomials of degree ≥ 2 in N, e.g. N² ≡ q²
   492	    (mod p−1), whose curvature in p is larger (multimod Thm A′ handles the balanced
   493	    degree ≥ 2 case);
   494	  - multi-dimensional collision searches;
   495	  - lattice / Coppersmith decoding;
   496	  - polynomial-evaluation methods (PS);
   497	  - class groups;
   498	  - subexponential methods (heuristic or rigorous-randomised).
   499	- **Cost model.** X counts babies plus giants, i.e. window comparisons. A computation that
   500	  processed a structured family of windows implicitly is not excluded.
   501	
   502	## 6. Attempts to beat the R^{1/6} penalty (candidate cards, condensed)
   503	
   504	All are evaluated at intermediate R = N^{θ}, 0 < θ < 1/3.
   505	
   506	| # | Modification | Outcome | Label |
   507	|---|---|---|---|
   508	| 1 | Anisotropic weights (A, B) with B/A ≈ R and A = a > 1 (rational weights, multipliers kN) | span ÷ a but instances × a. Cost Δ^{2/3}q^{1/3}a^{1/3}, minimised at a = 1. In Prop O′, β = A ≥ 1 | refuted for this model |
   509	| 2 | Composite / CRT locks ℓ₁ℓ₂ \| Bp − Aq | M cancels in g″ (Prop O′(a) is M-free). Each giant covers the same number of candidates of a smaller class | refuted for this model |
   510	| 3 | q-side extraction (exponent 1 + RN ≡ q + Rp mod q−1) | Same target value Rp + q, same window. In q-units the per-giant coverage is ×R and so is the candidate count | refuted for this model |
   511	| 4 | Finer ratio sub-bands, one list each | Per sub-band Δ^{4/3}(p/R)^{1/3}, × R/Δ sub-bands ⇒ Δ^{1/3}(Rq)^{1/3}, worse | refuted (computed) |
   512	| 5 | Coarse small weights (1, B ≤ H²), full band window (GPT-6.1-sol) | Same exponent H^{4/3}q^{1/3}, simpler. Now the main construction | proved (§3.1), no gain |
   513	| 6 | Composite moduli for multiplicity (Σ_M 1/M ≍ 1 per dyadic range vs 1/n for primes) | Coverage per target is still ≈ 1/M. Re-optimising gives λ^{2/3}q^{1/3} independent of the modulus density | refuted (computed; consistent with Prop O′) |
   514	| 7 | Lucas (p+1), cyclotomic Φ_k(p) handles | p+1: N ≡ −q, target −(Bp + Aq), same family. Cyclotomic: cycloharvey D1 (no new targets) | refuted for this model |
   515	| 8 | Exponents of degree ≥ 2 in N (targets with q², …) | g″ ≍ q²/p² ≫ R/p, larger curvature. No construction beats linear | not proved in general (outside 𝔏) |
   516	| 9 | Coppersmith / lattice windows | Need p mod M with M ≳ p^{1/2}. Locks give p mod ℓ only, and for R ≥ ℓ every class is hit (no information). Guessing costs ≥ √p | no gain found (heuristic accounting) |
   517	| 10 | Pollard–Strassen on lock classes | Must cover a union of classes of density ≍ 1 ⇒ √p | no gain (computed) |
   518	| 11 | McKee–Pinch pigeonhole (λp ≡ ±μq mod k, λμ ≤ k) | Guaranteed lock at k ≈ N^{1/3} ⇒ deterministic N^{1/3}. Worse | no gain |
   519	| 12 | HH 2022 local lattices | Accounting S + √N/S^{3/2} with A = O(√(S/R)) recovers N^{1/5} at best (GPT-6.1-sol) | no gain |
   520	| 13 | Structured (non-interval) baby sets exploiting the common curvature g″ = 2N/p³ of all A = 1 targets | Prop O′ allows arbitrary sets and gives the same bound | refuted for this model (new in O′) |
   521	
   522	**Conclusion.** Every attempt either lies in 𝔏, where Prop O′ makes q^{1/3} optimal, or
   523	costs more. Beating the penalty needs a mechanism outside 𝔏: non-linear targets with
   524	cancelling curvature, multi-dimensional collisions, lattices, or an average-case selection

