    21	  A ≤ H/√R, B ≤ H√R (R = 2^j ≤ H²/16) has the same exponent. It costs N^{1/6+5ε/6}·polylog
    22	  and fails on O(N^{−ε}) of the pairs with q/p ≤ N^{ε}. Every window span stays
    23	  ≤ (1+√2)H√N, so the primes, γ, the four blocks and the baby list are D′'s. Only the target
    24	  count grows, by the number of bands, O(n). The large-sieve, clean-band and FULL-count
    25	  lemmas carry over with H replaced by H² in one gcd bound.
    26	- **Theorem U_θ (proved; regime II).** For H²/16 < R ≤ N^{θ}, θ ≤ 1/5 − 3ε, use weights
    27	  (1, B) with 2 ≤ B ≤ H² (a construction suggested by GPT-6.1-sol, simpler than our
    28	  pre-registered one) and a per-band prime scale Y_j ≍ (NR_j)^{1/6}H^{−2/3}. A band costs
    29	  H^{4/3}(NR)^{1/6}, so the exponent is (1+θ)/6 + 2ε/3. Our pre-registered fine-band
    30	  design W2 (weights (1, B) with B ≈ R) has the same exponent (Variant U_θ′, proved).
    31	- **Profile (proved, with Pollard–Strassen as prior art).** At ratio N^{θ} the best exponent
    32	  we have is e(θ) = min((1+θ)/6, (1−θ)/4) (+O(ε) on the DCL side). So:
    33	  - it exceeds 1/6 for every 0 < θ < 1/3;
    34	  - it is below 1/5 for every θ ≠ 1/5;
    35	  - its maximum is 1/5, at θ = 1/5.
    36	- **Proposition O′ (proved in its model; the strongest negative result here).** Take any
    37	  one-dimensional collision search: any number of baby lists with *arbitrary* exponent sets,
    38	  giants with Lehman-linear targets αp + βq + c₀, any lock moduli (prime, composite, CRT),
    39	  either side extracted, multipliers, rational weights. Covering a fraction f of the integer
    40	  candidates of ratio band R costs ≥ c·min(√(fp), f^{2/3}q^{1/3}).
    41	  - At R = N^{1/5} this is ≥ c f^{2/3}N^{1/5}. **In this model the 1/5 peak cannot be
    42	    removed.**
    43	  - The bound is tight on both branches: Theorem U_θ (q^{1/3}) and Pollard–Strassen (√p).
    44	  - Its scope is FULL coverage of the candidates, not average-case success. An almost-all
    45	    scheme could beat it only if its covered sets favoured the true factor beyond their
    46	    density. Non-linear targets, lattice methods and multi-dimensional searches are also
    47	    outside it (§5.3).
    48	- **Populations.**
    49	  - For the bounded-ratio population S_n, and per ratio band up to N^{o(1)}: N^{1/6+o(1)}.
    50	  - Uniform over semiprimes with both primes ≥ N^{1/3}: the exponent θ has a continuous law
    51	    on [0, 1/3]. Cost N^{1/6+η} handles only ≈ 30η of them, and there is **no**
    52	    almost-all exponent below 1/5. Exponent 1/5 − η′ still handles all but ≈ 30η′.
    53	  - Counted by N ≤ X over all semiprimes, almost all have p < X^{1/3}. Pollard–Strassen
    54	    already gives N^{1/6} for 1 − o(1) of them, so that measure is trivial. But it gives no
    55	    power-saving exceptional set, since a ≍ 1/log log N fraction has θ near 1/5.
    56	- **Beating R^{1/6}: no escape found.** We tried composite locks, q-side extraction,
    57	  multipliers kN, rational or anisotropic weights, finer sub-band lists, composite moduli for
    58	  multiplicity, Lucas/cyclotomic handles, Coppersmith windows, PS on lock classes, and the
    59	  McKee–Pinch pigeonhole. Each is either inside Proposition O′'s model, or costs more (§6).
    60	- **Experiments (pre-registered, run exactly as frozen).** §7. ⟨filled after runs⟩
    61	- **Extraction remark (GPT-6.1-sol, labelled conditional).** Harvey–Hittmeir 2026 gives a
    62	  base of order > m mod N deterministically. With seven blocks and exact-equality recovery,
    63	  that would remove D′'s order and FULL exceptional sets entirely, leaving only coverage
    64	  (§3.4).
    65	
    66	## 1. Populations and notation
    67	
    68	- n is the bit length, N ∈ [2^{n−1}, 2^n).
    69	- **Ratio band j:** S_{n,j} = {(p, q) primes : 2^j p < q ≤ 2^{j+1}p, 2^{n−1} ≤ pq < 2^n}.
    70	  Write R = R_j = 2^j. Then p ≍ P_j := √(N/R) and q ≍ Q_j := √(NR) (within factors √2).
    71	- S_n^{θ} = ∪_{j ≤ θn} S_{n,j} is the set of pairs with q/p ≤ N^{θ} roughly.
    72	  - j = 0 is D′'s population S_n, up to the boundary q = 2p.
    73	- **Counts (PNT, uniform for j ≤ n/3):** |S_{n,j}| ≥ c₀ 2^n/n². Choose p ∈ [x, 1.1x],
    74	  q ∈ [y, 1.1y] with xy = 0.6·2^n and y/x = 1.5·2^j. Both x, y ≥ 2^{n/3}, so each
    75	  progression has ≫ x/n, y/n primes.
    76	- Exceptions are **counted by pairs**, per band. Because |E_j| ≤ C2^{−εn}|S_{n,j}| for
    77	  every band, the union over bands is also an O(2^{−εn}) fraction of S_n^{θ}.
    78	
    79	## 2. Theorem U (regime I: R ≤ H²/16)
    80	
    81	### 2.1 Algorithm DCL-wide-I_{n,ε} (= `algorithm.dclw` with `params.theorem_design_U`)
    82	
    83	All parameters are D′'s (dcltheorem §2.1):
    84	- H = ⌈n2^{εn/2}⌉;
    85	- Y = ⌈2^{n/6}H^{−1/3}⌉;
    86	- four blocks 𝒫_k = {ℓ ≡ 3 (4) prime, 2^kY < ℓ ≤ 2^{k+1}Y};
    87	- base 2, L_k = ∏ℓ².
    88	
    89	**Change.** Let J be the largest j with 2^j ≤ H²/16. For each j ≤ J let
    90	
    91	  V_j = {(A, B) : 1 ≤ A ≤ a_j := ⌊H2^{−j/2}⌋, 1 ≤ B ≤ b_j := ⌊H2^{j/2}⌋, gcd(A,B) = 1, A ≠ B}.
    92	
    93	- The instance (A, B, j) carries the ratio interval (2^j, 2^{j+1}].
    94	- Its window is computed over the integer p-range x ∈ [x_lo, x_hi] = {x : 2^j < N/x² ≤ 2^{j+1}}
    95	  (`ratio_range`, exact integers).
    96	- Lo = ⌊2√(ABN)⌋ and Hi = max_{x∈{x_lo,x_hi}} ⌈Bx + AN/x⌉. The rest of each block is D′
    97	  verbatim, with one shared baby list per block for all instances of all bands:
    98	  - targets for every ℓ and every instance;
    99	  - roots by a^{(ℓ+1)/4};
   100	  - c, g_lo, W;
   101	  - giants u_ℓ^{B+AN−c}γ^{−(g_lo+jm)};
   102	  - m = m̄_k, or the endpoint-competitive grid;
   103	  - product/remainder trees, gcd, validated outputs.
   104	- With J = 0 the target list is exactly D′'s (test `test_band0_reproduces_dprime_targets`).
   105	
   106	### 2.2 Lemmas (changes to D′'s proof only)
   107	
   108	Fix (p, q) ∈ S_{n,j}, j ≤ J. Write R = 2^j.
   109	
   110	- **U1 (span).** For x in band j's p-range,
   111	  F(x) = Bx + AN/x ≤ b_j√(N/R) + a_j√(2RN) ≤ (1+√2)H√N, and Lo ≥ 0.
   112	  So Hi − Lo ≤ (1+√2)H√N + 2 ≤ span̄. This is D′'s span̄ (5/2)H(2^{n/2}+1) + 2H + 3.
   113	  Hence m̄_k is unchanged. (GPT-6.1-sol: with the exact interval minimum the real span is
   114	  ≤ (√2−1)H√N; we use the cruder bound, as the code does.)
   115	- **U2 (weights).**
   116	  - a_j b_j ≤ H²;
   117	  - |AB′ − A′B| ≤ a_j b_j ≤ H² < Y < ℓ;
   118	  - |V_j| ≥ c₂H². For a_j ≥ 4: a_j ≥ H/(2√R) and b_j ≥ H√R/2. Coprime pairs in [1,a]×[1,b]
   119	    number ≥ b·Σ_{A≤a}φ(A)/A − O(a log a) ≥ c·ab, and A = B removes ≤ a.
   120	  - |A − B| ≤ b_j ≤ H√R ≤ H².
   121	- **Lemma 1 (lock), Lemmas 2–5, Lemma 7 (FULL ⇔ d | (A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ) hold verbatim.**
   122	  - They only use ℓ ∤ ABN, ℓ < p and the window containing F(p).
   123	  - ℓ ≤ 16Y ≪ √(N/R) ≤ p, and ℓ > Y > H² ≥ A, B.
   124	  - The window contains F(p) because N/p² = q/p ∈ (2^j, 2^{j+1}].
   125	- **Lemma 6 (clean block)** needs Y⁴ > q. Here q ≤ √(2^{n}·2^{J+1}) ≤ H·2^{n/2}, and
   126	  Y⁴ ≥ 2^{2n/3}H^{−4/3} > H2^{n/2} for ε < 1/14 and n ≥ n₀.
   127	- **Lemma 9 (coverage, large sieve) holds uniformly in j.**
   128	  - Fix a prime q and let I_q = {p ∈ Z : q/2^{j+1} ≤ p < q/2^j}, of length ≤ q/(2R) + 1.
   129	  - By U2, the |V_j| classes AqB^{−1} mod ℓ are distinct. So the uncovered
   130	    U_k(q) ⊂ I_q avoids |V_j| classes mod every ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k.
   131	  - Montgomery–Vaughan (prime terms only, exactly as in D′) gives
   132	    |U_k(q)| ≤ (|I_q| + 1 + 4^{k+1}Y²)·n/(c₁H²).
   133	- **Lemma 10 (FULL count)** holds with g′ = gcd(A−B, d) ≤ H² (instead of ≤ H):
   134	  |F_k(q)| ≤ 2|V_j||𝒫_k| m̄_k (|I_q|H²/(2^kY·T) + 1).
   135	
   136	### 2.3 Theorem U
   137	
   138	**Theorem U (proved).** Fix ε ∈ (0, 1/60]. For n ≥ n₀(ε), DCL-wide-I_{n,ε} has these
   139	properties.
   140	- It outputs a proper divisor or FAIL on every input.
   141	- It costs O(n^{c}·2^{(1/6+5ε/6)n}) bit operations on every n-bit input.
   142	- It fails on at most C·2^{−εn}|S_{n,j}| pairs of each band j ≤ J, where 2^J ≥ H²/32 ≥ 2^{εn}.
   143	
   144	In particular it fails on O(N^{−ε}) of the pairs with q/p ≤ N^{ε}.
   145	
   146	*Proof.*
   147	- **(i) Correctness.** Identical to D′.
   148	- **(ii) Cost.** Per block, the targets number ≤ 2Σ_j|V_j||𝒫_k| ≤ 2(J+1)H²·2^kY with
   149	  J + 1 ≤ 2log₂H + 1 = O(n). The list length is m̄_k + T′ with T′ ≤ T + Σ W/m. Every other
   150	  row of D′'s cost table scales by at most J + 1. So the cost is O(n^{O(1)}(HP/Y² + nH²Y)),
   151	  i.e. O(n^{O(1)}H^{5/3}2^{n/6}).
   152	- **(iii) Exceptions, per band j**, with T = ⌊2^{(1/4−ε)n}⌋. The failing pairs lie in
   153	  E_a ∪ E_b ∪ E_c, as in D′. For each, sum over the primes q of the band, at most
   154	  C√(NR)/n of them, and divide by |S_{n,j}| ≥ c₀2^n/n².
   155	  - **E_a (ord_q(2) ≤ T).** By Lemma 8, ≤ T(T+1)/(n−1) bad q, each with ≤ |I_q| + 1 ≤
   156	    C√(N/R) partners. Fraction ≤ C n T²/√(NR) ≤ C n 2^{−2εn}.
   157	  - **E_b (uncovered in the clean block).** Σ_q |U| ≤ (C√(NR)/n)(C√(N/R) + 256Y²)·n/(c₁H²)
   158	    ≤ C 2^n/H², because Y² ≤ √(N/R) when R ≤ H². Fraction ≤ C n²/H² ≤ C 2^{−εn}.
   159	  - **E_c (FULL).** Using |𝒫_k| ≤ 2^kY, m̄_k ≤ 3H√N/(4^kY²) and |I_q| ≤ C√(N/R):
   160	    Σ_q|F_k| ≤ (C√(NR)/n)·2H²·2^kY·m̄_k·(C√(N/R)H²/(2^kYT) + 1). Dividing by |S_{n,j}|:
   161	
   162	      ≤ C n (H⁵√N/(Y²T) + H⁴/Y) ≤ n^{O(1)}(2^{(−1/12 + 23ε/6)n} + 2^{(−1/6+3ε)n}),
   163	
   164	    using √R ≤ H, Y² ≥ 2^{n/3}H^{−2/3}, T ≥ 2^{(1/4−ε)n−1} and H ≤ 2n2^{εn/2}. Compared
   165	    with D′, the extra factor is one H, coming from g′ ≤ H². For ε ≤ 1/60,
   166	    −1/12 + 23ε/6 ≤ −ε − 1/360, so this is O(2^{−εn}) for n ≥ n₀(ε) (see the constant
   167	    note).
   168	- **Union.** The total is O(2^{−εn}) per band. ∎
   169	
   170	*Constant note.* D′'s own proof needs ε < 1/52 at this step, and ours needs ε < 1/58. We
   171	state Theorem U for ε ∈ (0, 1/60], where 29ε/6 ≤ 29/360 < 1/12. Then the first term is
   172	n^{O(1)}2^{−(1/12−29ε/6)n}·2^{−εn}, and 1/12 − 29/360 = 1/360 > 0 absorbs the
   173	polynomial factor for n ≥ n₀(ε). So no change of range is needed.
   174	
   175	**Corollary U″ (polylog).** With H = ⌈n^{1+a}⌉, as in D″, the algorithm covers ratios up to
   176	n^{2+2a}/32. It costs N^{1/6}(log N)^{O(1+a)} and fails on O((log N)^{−2a}) per band. The
   177	proof is the same.
   178	
   179	## 3. Theorem U_θ (regime II: H²/16 < R ≤ N^{θ}, θ ≤ 1/5 − 3ε)
   180	
   181	### 3.1 The coarse construction (theorem design; GPT-6.1-sol `unbalanced_1.out` §2)
   182	
   183	Put h = H² (D′'s H). For each regime-II band j (J < j ≤ J_θ := ⌊θn⌋):
   184	- **Weights.** V′ = {(1, B) : 2 ≤ B ≤ h}, with the band's ratio interval (2^j, 2^{j+1}].
   185	  - So |V′| = h − 1 ≥ h/2.
   186	  - The classes qB^{−1} mod ℓ are distinct for ℓ > h.
   187	  - |A − B| = B − 1 < h.
   188	- **Prime scale.** Y_j = ⌈(2^{(n+j)/2}/h)^{1/3}⌉. This is D′'s balance Y ≍ (span/|V|)^{1/3}.
   189	  Blocks 𝒫_{j,k} = {ℓ ≡ 3 (4) : 2^kY_j < ℓ ≤ 2^{k+1}Y_j}, k = 0..3, each with its own
   190	  L, γ and baby list.
   191	- **Everything else is D′:** roots, c, windows over the band's p-range, giants, trees, gcds,
   192	  validation.
   193	- Not run as a frozen experiment. It is `params.WideDesign` with
   194	  `insts = [Inst(1, B, 2^j, 1, 2^{j+1}, 1, j) for B in 2..h]`.
   195	
   196	**U3 (span; proved).** Since B ≤ h < R, the minimiser √(N/B) of F(x) = Bx + N/x lies
   197	right of the band's p-range [x₁, x₂] ⊂ [√(N/2^{j+1}), √(N/2^j)]. So F is decreasing there,
   198	and
   199	
   200	  F(x₁) − F(x₂) ≤ N/x₁ − N/x₂ ≤ (√2 − 1)√(N2^j) + O(1).
   201	
   202	- The code's window [⌊2√(BN)⌋, F(x₁)] is wider. By AM–GM its width is
   203	  F(x₁) − 2√(BN) ≤ F(x₁) ≤ √(2N2^{j+1}) + B√(N/2^{j}) ≤ 3Q_j for n ≥ n₀.
   204	- We charge span̄_j := 3Q_j + O(1) with Q_j := 2^{(n+j+1)/2}, and m̄_{j,k} = ⌊span̄_j/(4^kY_j²)⌋ + 2.
   205	
   206	**Lemmas 1–7 hold verbatim** (same checks as §2.2):
   207	- ℓ > Y_j > h ≥ B. Indeed Y_j ≥ (2^{n/2}/h)^{1/3} > h for ε < 1/8.
   208	- ℓ ≤ 16Y_j < x₁. Indeed Y_j ≤ (2^{(n+θn)/2})^{1/3} ≪ 2^{(n−θn)/2}/4 for θ < 1/2.
   209	- Lemma 6: Y_j⁴ > √2 q. Here Y_j⁴ ≥ (Q_j/(2h))^{4/3} > 2Q_j because Q_j^{1/3} > 4h^{4/3}.
   210	
   211	**Theorem U_θ (proved).** Fix ε ∈ (0, 1/60] and θ ∈ (0, 1/5 − 3ε]. For n ≥ n₀(ε, θ), the
   212	algorithm DCL-wide_{n,ε,θ} has these properties. It runs regime I (§2) and then the coarse
   213	construction for bands J < j ≤ ⌊θn⌋, on every input.
   214	- (i) It outputs only proper divisors or FAIL.
   215	- (ii) It costs n^{O(1)}·(H^{5/3}2^{n/6} + H^{4/3}2^{(1+θ)n/6}), i.e.
   216	  N^{1/6 + max(5ε/6, θ/6 + 2ε/3)}·(log N)^{O(1)} bit operations, on every n-bit input.
   217	- (iii) It fails on O(2^{−εn})·|S_{n,j}| pairs of each band j ≤ θn, hence on O(N^{−ε}) of
   218	  S_n^{θ}.
   219	
   220	*Proof.* Regime-I bands are Theorem U. Fix a regime-II band j; write R = 2^j, Y = Y_j,
   221	P_j = √(N/R), Q_j ≍ √(NR).
   222	
   223	- **(ii) Cost per block.**
   224	  - Targets ≤ 2h·2^{k}Y.
   225	  - The list is m̄_{j,k} + T′ ≤ 3·span̄_j/(4^kY²) + 2 + 2h2^{k+1}Y. This is
   226	    ≤ C(Q_j/Y² + hY) = C h^{2/3}Q_j^{1/3}.
   227	  - The exponent tree costs O(Y log Y) mults mod N. The remaining rows of D′'s table are
   228	    Õ(targets + list).
   229	  - Summing the geometric series over j ≤ θn gives C h^{2/3}(2^{n(1+θ)/2})^{1/3}n^{O(1)}.
   230	- **(iii) E_a.** T := ⌊2^{(n+j)/4}2^{−εn}⌋, i.e. ≍ Q_j^{1/2}N^{−ε}.
   231	  - Lemma 8 gives ≤ T(T+1)/(n−1) primes q > 2^{n/2−1} with ord_q(2) ≤ T, each with
   232	    ≤ |I_q| + 1 ≤ 2P_j partners.
   233	  - So |E_a| ≤ 2P_jT(T+1)/(n−1). Dividing by c₀2^n/n² gives ≤ C n T²/Q_j ≤ C n 2^{−2εn}.
   234	- **(iii) E_b (large sieve).** For a prime q of the band let I_q = [q/2^{j+1}, q/2^j) ∩ Z,
   235	  with |I_q| ≤ P_j + 1.
   236	  - In the clean block k(q), the uncovered p ∈ I_q avoid h − 1 distinct classes mod every
   237	    ℓ ∈ 𝒫_{j,k}.
   238	  - So |U| ≤ (|I_q| + 1 + 4^{k+1}Y²)/((h−1)Σ1/ℓ) ≤ C(P_j + Y²)n/h.
   239	  - Now Y² ≤ P_j ⇔ (NR)^{1/3}h^{−2/3} ≤ (N/R)^{1/2} ⇔ R^{5/6} ≤ N^{1/6}h^{2/3}, which holds
   240	    for θ ≤ 1/5.
   241	  - Summing over ≤ CQ_j/n primes q and dividing by c₀2^n/n²: fraction ≤ C n²/h = C2^{−εn}.
   242	- **(iii) E_c (FULL; D′ Lemma 10 with g′ = gcd(B−1, d) ≤ h).**
   243	  - Σ_q |F_k(q)| ≤ (CQ_j/n)·2h·2^kY·m̄_{j,k}·(CP_j h/(2^kY T) + 1).
   244	  - Divide by c₀2^n/n², using Q_jP_j ≍ N, m̄_{j,k} ≤ C Q_j/(4^kY²) and Y³ ≍ Q_j/h:
   245	
   246	      fraction ≤ C n (h² m̄/T + hR/Y + hQ_jY/N)
   247	               ≤ C n (h^{8/3}Q_j^{−1/6}2^{εn} + h^{4/3}R^{5/6}N^{−1/6} + N^{−1/6}).
   248	
   249	  - **First term.** With Q_j ≥ 2^{(n−1)/2} and h ≤ n²2^{εn}, it is
   250	    ≤ n^{O(1)}2^{(−1/12 + 11ε/3)n}. That is O(2^{−εn}) for ε < 1/56.
   251	  - **Second term.** It is ≤ n^{O(1)}2^{4εn/3}R^{5/6}2^{−n/6}, which is O(2^{−εn}) when
   252	    R ≤ 2^{(1/5 − 14ε/5)n − O(log n)}. That holds for θ ≤ 1/5 − 3ε and n ≥ n₀(ε, θ).
   253	    **This is where θ < 1/5 enters the success proof.** For larger R the two-sided "+1" CRT
   254	    term, multiplied by the number of (B, ℓ, δ), is too large.
   255	- **Union** over bands as in §1. ∎
   256	
   257	**What fails at θ ≥ 1/5.**
   258	- Y_j² > P_j, so the large sieve's Q² term dominates the interval (E_b fails).
   259	- E_c's second term exceeds 1.
   260	- The cost (1+θ)/6 is already above PS's (1−θ)/4.
   261	- These are three independent signals at the same place.
