The upper-bound constructions largely survive. The coarse construction needs a span repair. Proposition O′ is false in the stated model, although its nonconstant-target lower bound is repairable.

Source files were unchanged; §7 was excluded. Below, write \(X_0=2^n\), \(P=(X_0/R)^{1/2}\), \(Q=(X_0R)^{1/2}\), and \(h=H^2\).

1. **CORRECT — Theorem U’s substantive proof and epsilon range.**  
   [§2.2–2.3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:106)

   The span bound follows directly from
   \[
   Bx+AN/x\le (1+\sqrt2)H\sqrt N.
   \]
   The shared D′ span therefore suffices.

   Primitive distinct weights have nonzero determinant of absolute value at most \(a_jb_j\le H^2<\ell\), so their classes are distinct. Since \(a_j\ge4\), \(a_jb_j\gg H^2\), and \(b_j\ge H\), the stated coprime-pair estimate gives \(|V_j|\gg H^2\) uniformly. Excluding \(A=B\) is harmless.

   The scale separations hold: \(Y>H^2\), all block primes are smaller than \(p\), and
   \[
   \frac{Y^4}{H X_0^{1/2}}
   \gg n^{-7/3}X_0^{(1-7\varepsilon)/6}\longrightarrow\infty.
   \]
   Thus four blocks suffice.

   The large sieve is normalized correctly: count uncovered **integers**, sum over \(O(Q/n)\) prime \(q\)’s, and divide by \(|S_{n,j}|\gg X_0/n^2\). This gives \(O(n^2/H^2)\).

   The FULL count with \(g'\le H^2\) gives
   \[
   \frac{|E_c|}{|S_{n,j}|}
   \ll n\left(\frac{H^5X_0^{1/2}}{Y^2T}+\frac{H^4}{Y}\right).
   \]
   Its first exponent is exactly \(-1/12+23\varepsilon/6\). Moreover,
   \[
   -\frac1{12}+\frac{23\varepsilon}{6}
   +\varepsilon+\frac1{360}
   =\frac{29}{6}\left(\varepsilon-\frac1{60}\right)\le0.
   \]
   The claimed \(1/360\) margin is correct, including equality at \(\varepsilon=1/60\). Polynomial factors are absorbed.

   **Minor literal repairs:** line 123 reverses the comparison between \(p\) and \(\sqrt{N/R}\); use \(p\ge\sqrt{N/(2R)}\). Bounds involving the shared \(\bar m_k\) should consistently use \(X_0^{1/2}\), or an unspecified absolute constant times \(\sqrt N\).

2. **WRONG — High severity: the coarse construction assumes the wrong regime boundary.**  
   [U3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:196)

   Regime II starts at \(R>h/16\), whereas “\(B\le h<R\)” requires \(R>h\). Consequently, \(F\) need not decrease, and the charged \(3Q_j\) span can be insufficient.

   This persists asymptotically. Take \(\varepsilon=1/60\), \(n=120\cdot2^t\), the first regime-II band, \(B=h\), and \(N=X_0-1\). Then
   \[
   h/R=225/16,
   \]
   and the upper endpoint alone gives
   \[
   \mathrm{Hi}-\mathrm{Lo}
   \ge \left(\frac{121}{16}+o(1)\right)\sqrt{NR},
   \]
   exceeding \(3Q_j=(3\sqrt2+o(1))\sqrt{NR}\).

   **Exact repair preserving the weights:** discard the monotonicity argument and use
   \[
   F(x)\le(\sqrt2+h/R)\sqrt{NR}
   <(16+\sqrt2)\sqrt{NR}.
   \]
   Set
   \[
   \overline{\mathrm{span}}_j=20\sqrt{X_0R}+2
   \]
   and define \(\bar m_{j,k}\) accordingly. Every subsequent exponent remains unchanged.

   Alternatively, retain only \(2\le B\le\min(h,R)\); this preserves \(\gg h\) weights and makes the decreasing-function argument valid.

3. **CORRECT — The coarse construction’s remaining lemmas, exception arithmetic, and cost, after that repair.**  
   [§3.1 proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:206)

   Lemma 1’s conditions hold: \(\ell>h\ge B\), \(\ell<p\), and the window contains \(F(p)\). Also \(Y^4\gg Q\), since \(Q/h^4\to\infty\) throughout the stated epsilon range. Four blocks suffice.

   With \(T\asymp Q^{1/2}X_0^{-\varepsilon}\),
   \[
   |E_a|/|S_{n,j}|\ll nX_0^{-2\varepsilon}.
   \]
   Coverage gives
   \[
   |E_b|/|S_{n,j}|
   \ll \frac{n^2}{h}\left(1+\frac{Y^2}{P}\right),
   \qquad
   \frac{Y^2}{P}\asymp
   X_0^{-1/6}R^{5/6}h^{-2/3}.
   \]
   Hence \(Y^2\le P\) throughout \(\theta\le1/5\).

   The FULL fraction is bounded by
   \[
   Cn\left(
   h^{8/3}Q^{-1/6}X_0^\varepsilon
   +h^{4/3}R^{5/6}X_0^{-1/6}
   +h^{2/3}X_0^{-1/3}R^{2/3}
   \right).
   \]
   **Yes: the second term really is \(h^{4/3}R^{5/6}X_0^{-1/6}\).**

   The first term has exponent at most \(-1/12+11\varepsilon/3\). At \(\varepsilon=1/60\), its margin beyond \(X_0^{-\varepsilon}\) is \(1/180\). At \(\theta=1/5-3\varepsilon\), the second term has exponent \(-7\varepsilon/6\), leaving margin \(\varepsilon/6\). The third term is smaller than the stated \(X_0^{-1/6}\) bound.

   The cost remains
   \[
   n^{O(1)}h^{2/3}Q^{1/3}.
   \]

   **WRONG wording in “What fails”:** \(Y^2>P\) does not begin at \(\theta=1/5\). Its threshold is
   \[
   R\asymp X_0^{1/5}h^{4/5}.
   \]
   In particular, at \(R=X_0^{1/5}\), \(Y^2/P\asymp h^{-2/3}\ll1\).

4. **CORRECT — Fine-band span, coverage partition, and cost.**  
   [§3.2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:263)

   U4’s identity and bound are valid. On each \(I_{q,b}\), the common admissible \(B\)-interval has length at most \(2\Delta<\ell\), contains at least \(\Delta-1\) weights, and loses at most one multiple of \(\ell\). Thus \(\omega(\ell)\ge\Delta-2\), with distinct inverse classes.

   Summing the sieve bounds costs
   \[
   \frac{Cn}{\Delta}(P+RY^2).
   \]
   The needed comparison is
   \[
   \frac{RY^2}{P}
   \asymp\Delta^{4/3}X_0^{-1/6}R^{-1/6}=o(1).
   \]
   This holds uniformly for the stated regime and parameters. The exceptional fraction is \(O(n^2/\Delta)\).

   The balance and cost are also correct:
   \[
   Y\asymp\Delta^{2/3}X_0^{1/6}R^{-5/6},
   \qquad
   C_{\mathrm{band}}\ll n^{O(1)}\Delta^{2/3}(X_0R)^{1/6}.
   \]

5. **CORRECT — The swapped FULL count is valid with the stated theorem parameters.**  
   [Lemma 10′](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:282)

   Crucially, fixing \(p,B,\ell,\delta\) makes
   \[
   Z=(B-1)(p-1)+\ell^2\delta
   \]
   independent of \(q\). Here \(Z\) denotes the note’s \(X\).

   The chosen \(\Delta\) gives \(R/\Delta\ge4\cdot2^K\). With a public, bit-length-based \(\bar m_{j,k}\),
   \[
   \ell^2\bar m_{j,k}
   \le4\overline{\mathrm{span}}_j+8\,4^KY^2
   <Rp/2
   \]
   for sufficiently large \(n\). The first contribution is small because \(\Delta/R\) is small; the second tends to zero relative to \(Rp\). Therefore \(0<Z<3q<N\). The zero-divisor-count catastrophe \(Z=0\) is excluded.

   FULL implies \(e=\operatorname{ord}_q(2)>T\) divides \(Z\). Cleanliness gives \(\ell\nmid e\), so CRT with
   \[
   q\equiv1\pmod e,\qquad q\equiv Bp\pmod\ell
   \]
   gives at most \(2\Delta p/(T\ell)+1\) possibilities for each divisor \(e\). Multiplication by \(\tau(Z)\) is legitimate.

   Summation gives exactly the stated form
   \[
   Cn^2\tau_*
   \left(\frac{\bar m\Delta}{T}+\frac{\bar mRY}{Q}\right).
   \]
   More precisely, its two contributions are
   \[
   X_0^{o(1)}
   \left(
   \Delta^{5/3}X_0^{-1/12+\varepsilon}R^{-1/12}
   +\Delta^{4/3}X_0^{-1/6}R^{-1/6}
   \right).
   \]
   The displayed exponents in the note are valid upper bounds; its first is loose. The divisor-function factor is absorbed by the fixed epsilon margins.

   **Clarification to add:** explicitly carry over \(T\asymp Q^{1/2}X_0^{-\varepsilon}\) and specify that \(\bar m\) is public. Using an actual \(q\)-dependent window length while fixing \(\delta\) would invalidate this counting argument.

6. **CORRECT — \(O(1/\varepsilon)\) blocks suffice; four generally do not.**  
   [Clean-block discussion](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:294)

   The required condition is \(Y^K>q_{\max}\). At the largest permitted ratio,
   \[
   Y=X_0^{19\varepsilon/6+o(1)},\qquad q_{\max}\ll X_0^{3/5}.
   \]
   Thus the stated
   \[
   K=\left\lceil\frac3{5\varepsilon}\right\rceil+2
   \]
   is more than sufficient. The resulting \(2^{O(1/\varepsilon)}\) constant is correctly acknowledged.

   An order \(1/\varepsilon\) block count is needed by this product argument near the upper endpoint as \(\varepsilon\to0\). It is unnecessary in smaller-ratio subranges. Four blocks suffice asymptotically only when
   \[
   \theta<(1+16\varepsilon)/23.
   \]

7. **CORRECT — Proposition O′(a), including arbitrary exponent sets.**  
   [Proof of (a)](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:430)

   On the right of the band minimum, \(g'(x_0)\ge0\), including when the minimum is the left endpoint. Therefore
   \[
   g'\ge\kappa(x-x_0),\qquad
   g-m_0\le(x-x_0)g'\le g'^2/\kappa.
   \]
   The derivative-height inequality uses only \(g''\ge\kappa\) on the band.

   The per-value preimage bounds and summation over integer-spaced values are valid. The constant \(8\) is safe. Negating a concave target reverses interval endpoint conventions, which does not change the final bound.

8. **WRONG — Fatal model counterexample to Proposition O′(b) and (c).**  
   [Model definition](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:394)

   Nothing excludes \(\alpha=\beta=0\). Choose
   \[
   M=1,\quad r=0,\quad c_0=g_0=0,\quad S=\{0\},
   \]
   with one giant. Then \(g(p)=0\) for every candidate, integrality refinement removes nothing, and **full coverage costs \(2\)**.

   Taking \(R=N^{1/5}\) yields a counterexample family to the claimed \(N^{1/5}\) lower bound.

   **Exact repair:** require \((\alpha,\beta)\ne(0,0)\). Then, for \(\beta=0\), the refined class is empty or one arithmetic progression with modulus \(M'\), and
   \[
   g(r'+M'x)=c+kx,\qquad
   k=\alpha M'/M^2\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}.
   \]
   Part (b) follows. The aggregation in (c) then correctly gives the stated minimum.

   The floor relaxation is coherent as a combinatorial definition after making the \(\beta=0\) integrality restriction explicit. “Exactly collides” should become “has an intended index collision”: collisions modulo a group order are deliberately excluded. General targets also need an explicit lock-admissibility condition if the model is presented as describing executable searches.

9. **GAP — High severity: “optimal in the model” is not established by the cited upper bounds.**  
   [Corollary O″](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:460)

   The repaired proposition proves a lower bound for constant-fraction candidate coverage **for each fixed \(N\)**. Theorem Uθ proves coverage of the true factor for most varying semiprimes. Its sieve argument fixes \(q\), not \(N\). The multiplicity \(\lambda\asymp H^2/n\) does not bridge these quantifiers.

   Pollard–Strassen is also outside the stated model. The combinatorial \(\sqrt p\) branch can instead be matched explicitly using \(M=1,\alpha=1,\beta=0\), one interval baby set, and translated intervals covering the candidate range.

   **Exact repair:** delete the claimed DCL constant-fraction coverage assertion and replace “optimal in \(\mathfrak L\)” with “the lower-bound exponents coincide with the available factoring upper-bound exponents, under different coverage guarantees,” unless a matching fixed-\(N\) construction is supplied.

10. **CORRECT population arithmetic; WRONG impossibility inference and one counting constant.**  
    [§4](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:361)

    The normalized density and CDF are
    \[
    \rho(\theta)=\frac{2}{\log2}\frac1{1-\theta^2},
    \qquad
    F(t)=\log_2\frac{1+t}{1-t}.
    \]
    The small-\(\eta\) covered fraction is
    \[
    F(6\eta)+1-F(1/3-4\eta)
    =\frac{21}{\log2}\eta+O(\eta^2).
    \]
    The quoted \(0.151\) and \(0.301\) values check out. Near the peak, the missing fraction is
    \[
    \frac{125}{6\log2}\eta'+O(\eta'^2)\approx30.056\,\eta'.
    \]

    These quantify where the **proved cost guarantees** apply. They do not establish that no almost-all algorithm achieves a smaller exponent. Replace the unqualified impossibility statements accordingly.

    For all semiprimes up to \(X\), the correct fixed-\(\delta\) asymptotic is
    \[
    \Pr(p>X^\delta)\sim
    \frac{\log((1-\delta)/\delta)}{\log\log X},
    \qquad 0<\delta<1/2,
    \]
    rather than \(\log(1/(2\delta))/\log\log X\).

    Also distinguish fixed-\(\varepsilon\) power-saving exceptions from taking \(\varepsilon\to0\) to obtain exponent \(1/6+o(1)\); the latter does not retain a fixed power saving from these estimates.

11. **CORRECT extraction principle; GAP in the operational specification.**  
    [§3.4](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:331)

    The cited 2026 theorem has the stated hypotheses and complexity. [Harvey–Hittmeir, Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2601.11131v2#Thmtheorem1.1)

    The prime-order inference is correct. If \(s=\operatorname{ord}_p(\gamma)<m\), the scan at \(i=s\) produces a proper gcd or \(N\); the latter implies \(\operatorname{ord}_N(\gamma)\mid s\), contradicting the certified order bound. The same applies to \(q\).

    The exact-equality test is also correct. The true giant’s matching baby index is unique modulo \(p\), so it reconstructs \(T=Bp+Aq\); the quadratic has root \(p\).

    **Complete the algorithm as follows:**

    - Use \(D\ge\max_k m_k\) for a shared base.
    - Scan \(1\le i<m\). Return a proper gcd; skip a block if a gcd equals \(N\). This prevents repeated babies from creating quadratically many sorting matches on bad blocks.
    - Test and remove every giant having an exact baby equality.
    - On a remaining product gcd \(N\), scan that giant’s \(m\) differences and return a proper factor.
    - Bound uncovered pairs by the union over the seven possible selected blocks, because cleanliness now depends on both primes.

    The clean block cannot be skipped, and the extra work remains within the claimed budget. Exact-match removal and subsequent collision recovery follow the established pattern in [Harvey, Algorithms 4.1–4.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450#S4).

12. **No verified uniform power improvement near \(\theta=1/5\) follows, but the exclusions need narrower wording.**  
    [§6](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:502)

    After excluding constant targets, the repaired curvature argument genuinely prevents a power improvement for constant-fraction structural coverage in this model.

    There are concrete qualifications:

    - HH extraction removes the FULL restriction and allows the coarse coverage analysis through \(R\asymp X_0^{1/5}h^{4/5}\). It does not improve the cost peak.
    - The finite-\(H\) cost crossover is
      \[
      R_*=X_0^{1/5}h^{-8/5}=X_0^{1/5}H^{-16/5}.
      \]
    - Factor-dependent structure can beat the profile on special inputs. For example, if \(p-1\) is \(B\)-smooth, an exponent containing all relevant prime powers gives a Pollard \(p-1\) test in \(\widetilde O(B)\) work whenever the other prime does not also annihilate the base. This beats the peak when \(B<N^{1/5}\), but supplies no required almost-all density theorem.

    Thus the note has no general exclusion of correlated average-case designs. The constant-target counterexample beats its formal model as written; it does not supply a factoring algorithm.

