1. **CORRECT — Theorem U’s substantive proof and epsilon range.**  
   [§2.2–2.3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:106)

   The span bound follows directly from
   \[
   Bx+AN/x\le (1+\sqrt2)H\sqrt N.
   \]
   The shared D′ span therefore suffices.

   Primitive distinct weights have nonzero determinant of absolute value at most \(a_jb_j\le H^2<\ell\), so their classes are distinct. Since \(a_j\ge4\), \(a_jb_j\gg H^2\), and \(b_j\ge H\), the stated coprime-pair estimate gives \(|V_j|\gg H^2\) uniformly. Excluding \(A=B\) is harmless.

   The scale separations hold: \(Y>H^2\), all block primes are smaller than \(p\), and
   \[
   \frac{Y^4}{H X_0^{1/2}}
   \gg n^{-7/3}X_0^{(1-7\varepsilon)/6}\longrightarrow\infty.
   \]
   Thus four blocks suffice.

   The large sieve is normalized correctly: count uncovered **integers**, sum over \(O(Q/n)\) prime \(q\)’s, and divide by \(|S_{n,j}|\gg X_0/n^2\). This gives \(O(n^2/H^2)\).

   The FULL count with \(g'\le H^2\) gives
   \[
   \frac{|E_c|}{|S_{n,j}|}
   \ll n\left(\frac{H^5X_0^{1/2}}{Y^2T}+\frac{H^4}{Y}\right).
   \]
   Its first exponent is exactly \(-1/12+23\varepsilon/6\). Moreover,
   \[
   -\frac1{12}+\frac{23\varepsilon}{6}
   +\varepsilon+\frac1{360}
   =\frac{29}{6}\left(\varepsilon-\frac1{60}\right)\le0.
   \]
   The claimed \(1/360\) margin is correct, including equality at \(\varepsilon=1/60\). Polynomial factors are absorbed.

   **Minor literal repairs:** line 123 reverses the comparison between \(p\) and \(\sqrt{N/R}\); use \(p\ge\sqrt{N/(2R)}\). Bounds involving the shared \(\bar m_k\) should consistently use \(X_0^{1/2}\), or an unspecified absolute constant times \(\sqrt N\).

