5. **CORRECT — The swapped FULL count is valid with the stated theorem parameters.**  
   [Lemma 10′](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md:282)

   Crucially, fixing \(p,B,\ell,\delta\) makes
   \[
   Z=(B-1)(p-1)+\ell^2\delta
   \]
   independent of \(q\). Here \(Z\) denotes the note’s \(X\).

   The chosen \(\Delta\) gives \(R/\Delta\ge4\cdot2^K\). With a public, bit-length-based \(\bar m_{j,k}\),
   \[
   \ell^2\bar m_{j,k}
   \le4\overline{\mathrm{span}}_j+8\,4^KY^2
   <Rp/2
   \]
   for sufficiently large \(n\). The first contribution is small because \(\Delta/R\) is small; the second tends to zero relative to \(Rp\). Therefore \(0<Z<3q<N\). The zero-divisor-count catastrophe \(Z=0\) is excluded.

   FULL implies \(e=\operatorname{ord}_q(2)>T\) divides \(Z\). Cleanliness gives \(\ell\nmid e\), so CRT with
   \[
   q\equiv1\pmod e,\qquad q\equiv Bp\pmod\ell
   \]
   gives at most \(2\Delta p/(T\ell)+1\) possibilities for each divisor \(e\). Multiplication by \(\tau(Z)\) is legitimate.

   Summation gives exactly the stated form
   \[
   Cn^2\tau_*
   \left(\frac{\bar m\Delta}{T}+\frac{\bar mRY}{Q}\right).
   \]
   More precisely, its two contributions are
   \[
   X_0^{o(1)}
   \left(
   \Delta^{5/3}X_0^{-1/12+\varepsilon}R^{-1/12}
   +\Delta^{4/3}X_0^{-1/6}R^{-1/6}
   \right).
   \]
   The displayed exponents in the note are valid upper bounds; its first is loose. The divisor-function factor is absorbed by the fixed epsilon margins.

   **Clarification to add:** explicitly carry over \(T\asymp Q^{1/2}X_0^{-\varepsilon}\) and specify that \(\bar m\) is public. Using an actual \(q\)-dependent window length while fixing \(\delta\) would invalidate this counting argument.

